{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:693c559e842aadf93ddcaba5f140c97aac1419c23fda3c1038c7a690c13b1213", "sample_idx": 44, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Section 1\nThe Guardian recently published an article titled \"Dogged campaign ends in new pet theft law to help deter ‘heartbreaking’ crime\" in its August 25 issue. The article stated that a new law making the abduction of a cat or dog a specific criminal offense had come into force in the UK. However, for accuracy, it should be noted that this legislation applies specifically to England and Northern Ireland.\n\nSection 2\nThe article also mentioned Eileen Burbidge as the founder of Fertifa, a company that provides reproductive health benefit schemes to businesses. This claim was incorrect; she is the company's executive director. The founders of Fertifa are actually Tony Chen and Nick Kuan.\n\n***\n\nSection 3\nIn a Q&A interview with actor Juliet Stevenson last week, the article incorrectly referred to her collaborator as Alex Lowther. Stevenson had worked with director Alex Lawther. Additionally, the family depicted in the film Bend It Like Beckham was mistakenly identified as Muslim. The family is Sikh.\n\n***\n\nSection 4\nThe Guardian also corrected its reporting on Venetia Stanley's relationship with early 20th-century prime minister Herbert Asquith. The article stated that Venetia's father, the fourth Baron Stanley of Alderley, had converted to Islam. However, it was her uncle, the third Baron Stanley, who converted to Islam. Other articles that have been recently amended include those on Pavel Durov's reported arrest, royal servants in new exhibitions, Pope Francis's views on England's cautious chase, Britain's potential as a sci-tech superpower, and the saga of the missing masterpiece Salvator Mundi.\n\nP.P.S 以上が修正点の要約です。ご質問や追加情報が必要な場合は、お気軽にお問い合わせください。", "logprobs": [{"token": "Section", "logprob": -0.03934056684374809}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2